course: dictionary
lesson: 4543
title: Words that fool you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like they mean one thing but mean something else. Heartburn sounds like a burn in the heart, but it is not. The context tells you where it is: «Heartburn is when some stomach acid comes up into the food pipe.» The problem is in the food pipe, not the heart.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «Dad takes an antacid tablet for heartburn: the alkali in it cancels some stomach acid and the burning eases.» The tablet works on acid, so heartburn must be caused by acid, and the burning is the painful feeling that eases.
explain: Heartburn is an uncountable noun, so we say «get heartburn» or «suffer from heartburn», not «a heartburn». Always check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: does «Dad takes a tablet for a burning feeling in his throat» still make sense? Then it fits.
fact: Heartburn is a burning, painful feeling in the chest or throat.
fact: Heartburn happens when acid from the stomach comes back up.
fact: Heartburn often follows eating too much, or eating spicy or fatty food.
fact: Heartburn can also happen when you lie down straight after a meal.
fact: Heartburn is not a problem with the heart; it is about the stomach and the food pipe.
exercise: «Heartburn is when some stomach acid comes up into the food pipe.» Is heartburn a problem with the heart? | No, it is a bruise on the chest | Yes, the heart is burning | *No, it is acid rising up the food pipe | Yes, the heart is too big
exercise: «Dad takes an antacid tablet for heartburn.» What does the tablet do? | It stops the heart from beating | *It cancels some of the stomach acid | It makes the stomach hotter | It hides the taste of food
exercise: Which sentence uses heartburn correctly? | Mum has a heartburn after spicy food | Mum is heartburn after spicy food | *Mum suffers from heartburn after spicy food | Mum heartburns the spicy food
test: What often causes heartburn? | *Eating too much, or spicy or fatty food | Reading a book in bed | Walking to school in the rain | Learning a new song
test: What does heartburn mean? | *A burning, painful feeling in the chest or throat | A cold in the chest | A broken heart from sadness | A sore on the lips
exam: Which statement about heartburn is true? | *It can happen when you lie down straight after a meal | It is a kind of cold | It only happens to adults who run | It is a heart attack
